Preta-Meend

Ingredients: INDIAN RAGA + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Bassoon
Creative Catalyst: The genre was born at an illegal rooftop rave in a flooded megacity, 2091.

Preta-Meend sounds like an antique orchestra drowning inside an abandoned municipal turbine: cavernous contrabassoons groan like bending steel rebar beneath sixty feet of black ocean water, while microtonal Hindustani raga scales slither across wet floorboards, haunted by mournful sargam vocal incantations and subterranean tape-decay.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: Glacial and elastic (48–66 BPM); unquantized 14-beat Dhamar and 10-beat Jhaptal cycles fractured by waterlogged sub-kicks, half-time hydraulic slaps, and rhythmic dripping textures that dissolve the downbeat into pure suspended tension.
- Harmonic Palette: Severe microtonal ragas of twilight and grief (Raga Bhairav, Marwa, Todi, and Ahir Bhairav); sharpened fourths (Tivra Ma), weeping flattened seconds (Komal Re), quarter-tone glissandos (meend), and dissonance anchored to submerged sub-octave drone roots.
- Production Signatures: Hydro-chamber re-amping (woodwinds and voice projected down drowned elevator shafts and recorded via hydrophones for sloshing subterranean reverb); Tape-drag Meend (analog tape loops of bassoon breath physically dragged to produce nauseating pitch drops mimicking structural collapse); Phantom-tracking tanpura (ring-modulated acoustic string drones that trigger bursts of bitcrushed white noise whenever microtonal scale thresholds are crossed).
- Dynamics: Begins in suffocating stillness with wheezing, unaccompanied reed murmurs (alap), stretches tension across vast plateaus of microtonal dread, detonates into sudden muddy percussion plunges, and exhales into damp, unresolved vapor.

Scene & Origin Lore
In 2091, the drowned towers of Greater Drowned Mumbai jutted from iridescent, chemical-slick tides like mossy monoliths. Ravers known as "Spire-Grafts" tethered flotillas of salvaged catamarans to eighty-story rooftop helipads, tapping derelict solar arrays to fuel illegal dusk-to-dawn assemblies called Jal-Jalsas. Dressed in algae-stained silk saris, emergency thermal mylar, and rebreather masks daubed with vermilion paste, this subculture gathered to mourn the drowned metropolis through hallucinatory communal listening. The sound was catalyzed when conservatory classical vocalists and scavenging woodwind players discovered that the bassoon’s low frequencies resonated in uncanny sympathetic vibration with the groan of the building's water-stressed foundation pilings, conjuring pretas—the hungry wandering ghosts of the submerged streets below.

Typical Instruments
- Modified Water-Bored Bassoon & Contrabassoon: Double-reed woodwinds retrofitted with extended PVC bell-extensions, brass bocal pickups, and microtonal pitch-pedals.
- Submerged Surbahar: Bass sitar with rust-treated bronze strings, bowed with horsehair to create weeping, metallic drones.
- Hydro-Tanpura: Mechanized drone boxes running microtonal loops through water canisters.
- Damped Pakhawaj & Duggi: Traditional barrel drums muffled with wet rags, producing low-end sub-thuds with extended pitch decay.
- Spectral Voice: Classical Hindustani khyal and dhrupad vocal acrobatics fed through tape-flutter delays and spectral vocoders.

